  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000122725,
      "SummaryText": "Natural gas and associated liquids enter the facility via pipeline from several Central Production\nFacilities. The natural gas and associated liquids enter the facility through a pig trap and a multi-finger slug catcher. Gas from the slug catcher is compressed by one of eight compressors, dehydrated by one of two glycol dehydration units, and a slip stream is sent to an amine unit to remove carbon dioxide before being sent to multiple sales points. The liquids from the slug catcher are routed to the liquids side of the process where any water is separated from the liquids and the liquids are stabilized in one of two stabilizer towers. The liquids flow through a caustic wash system to remove H2S, and a carbon filtration unit to treat the color (if needed) for pipeline quality purposes. The resulting NGL is then stored in pressurized bullet tanks prior to exiting the facility via pipeline or by truck (used during periods of pipeline shutdowns) to a third party gas plant for further processing.  Additionally ConocoPhillips processes stabilized NGL's received from other ConocoPhillips facilities. These third party NGL's are hauled to the plant via truck. They are unloaded into a pressurized tank and then are introduced into the ConocoPhillips process and are commingled with ConocoPhillips production (described above).\nNGL's is considered hazardous by the EPA. Because NGL's are pressurized flammable gas, it is\nnecessary to follow safety precautions during handling to prevent fire/explosion risks and reduce\nhealth threats to our workers and neighbors. ConocoPhillips has implemented various health and safety programs and processes and will strictly adhere to all Federal, State, and Local rules and regulations.\nSafety depends on:\n(a) The manner in which we handle this product\n(b) The safety devices inherent in the design of the facility\n(c) Operating and maintenance procedures, and\n(d) Employee training\nConocoPhillips Sugarloaf Station's emergency response program details e"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000122725,
      "SummaryText": "mployee responsibilities, actions and includes procedures for notification of the local fire authority and the LEPC to notify potentially affected neighbors.\nThe primary purpose of this facility is to:\n(a)\tReceive gas and remove and store Natural Gas Liquids from surrounding production wells;\n(b)\tRemove excess water and contaminants\n(c)\tTransfer gas product and natural gas liquids product to gas processors sales lines. This facility was designed and constructed in accordance with NFPA 58 and ASME codes and applicable API standards. A Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (SCADA) system has been\ninstalled and continually monitors each unit in the process as well by personnel stationed at the\nfacility. All employees are thoroughly trained on the characteristics and safe handling procedures for liquefied natural gas. Access is restricted to authorized facility employees, authorized management personnel and authorized contractors. ConocoPhillips Oil has implemented a variety of safe work practices and procedures to minimize risk to the employees and surrounding neighbors."
    }
  ]
